WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.230 --> 00:00:02.670 line:90%
Welcome to Academy Dialogues.

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:03.850 line:90%
It starts with us.

00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:05.610 line:84%
My name is Shawn
Finnie, and we're

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:07.830 line:84%
about to get into the
second in our continuing

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:11.760 line:84%
series of conversations about
race, ethnicity, identity,

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.790 line:84%
history, and the opportunity
and the art of filmmaking.

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:16.710 line:90%
Our theme today is Native Son.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:19.110 line:84%
Native Son was founded by
veteran magazine editor,

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:23.460 line:84%
cultural change-maker, and
LGBTQ activist Emil Wilbekin,

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:26.160 line:84%
with the intention to create
a safe space for Black gay men

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.670 line:84%
to celebrate our achievements,
build community, and inspire

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:30.730 line:90%
one another.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:32.490 line:84%
We are so grateful to
have Emil moderating

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:34.140 line:84%
today's conversation,
which will focus

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:36.780 line:84%
on growing the voice and the
visibility of Black gay men

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:39.300 line:84%
in Hollywood and
the film industry.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:40.930 line:84%
Emil has held
editor-in-chief positions

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:43.950 line:84%
at Vibe and Giant magazine,
been both managing

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:48.330 line:84%
editor and editor-at-large at
Essence, style guru at Complex,

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:52.020 line:84%
and chief content officer for
the annual Afropunk festival.

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:54.990 line:84%
Speaking with Emil are
writer/director/producer Lee

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:57.660 line:84%
Daniels and writer
Tarell Alvin McCraney,

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:00.390 line:84%
who have agreed to
share their stories.

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:02.040 line:90%
Lee directed the film Precious--

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:04.379 line:84%
Based on the Novel
"Push" by Sapphire.

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:06.660 line:84%
Precious received six
Oscar nominations,

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:09.900 line:84%
including Best Picture, and
took home two Oscars that night

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.540 line:84%
for Best Supporting
Actress and made history

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:15.810 line:84%
with the Best Adapted Screenplay
win, making Geoffrey Fletcher

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:18.120 line:84%
the first ever
African-American man

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:19.950 line:90%
to win an Oscar for writing.

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:22.740 line:84%
He also produced Monster's Ball,
for which Halle Berry became

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:25.230 line:84%
the first, and so
far only, Black woman

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:27.030 line:90%
to win a Best Actress Oscar.

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:30.510 line:84%
He also created the TV series
Empire and Star, both of which

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:32.670 line:90%
he produces and directs.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:35.130 line:84%
Tarell Alvin McCraney is
an Academy Award winner

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:38.700 line:84%
for Best Adapted Screenplay for
the film Moonlight, for which

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:42.090 line:84%
he co-wrote based on his play
In the Moonlight Black Boys Look

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:42.840 line:90%
Blue.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:45.990 line:84%
He also wrote High Flying Bird,
directed by Steven Soderbergh,

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:49.103 line:84%
which was released
on Netflix last year.

00:01:49.103 --> 00:01:51.270 line:84%
Thank you all for participating
in Academy Dialogues

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:52.710 line:90%
and for sharing your stories.

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:55.680 line:84%
We look forward to
your conversation.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:00.270 line:84%
Well, thank you, gentlemen,
for being here with us today.

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:04.080 line:84%
Our topic is the growing
voice and visibility

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:09.310 line:84%
of Black gay men in Hollywood
and in the film industry.

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:13.470 line:84%
You know, as Black gay men, we
live in triple consciousness.

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:15.990 line:84%
We're Black, we're
gay, and we're men.

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:20.610 line:84%
And particularly as we move
through the world right now,

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:25.350 line:84%
all of those things come into
play at every intersection.

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:29.790 line:84%
Native Son is framed after
James Baldwin's Notes

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:31.020 line:90%
of a Native Son.

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:35.250 line:84%
And it's very interesting that
within Notes of a Native Son

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:38.760 line:84%
he criticizes portrayals of
black people in Hollywood

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:42.450 line:84%
and notably talks a lot about
Dorothy Dandridge in Carmen

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:43.830 line:90%
Jones.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:47.970 line:84%
Why is it important that
we have this conversation

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:50.040 line:84%
about Black gay men
and our identity

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:52.830 line:84%
and our sexuality
and our masculinity

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:57.510 line:84%
and amplify our voice and
visibility in Hollywood?

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:59.220 line:90%
Lee, let's start with you.

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:03.750 line:84%
First off, what was the read on
Dorothy Dandridge, may I ask?

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:05.700 line:84%
What was he saying
about Dorothy Dandridge?

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:09.780 line:84%
It was more of how Black people
were depicted in Carmen Jones.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:12.780 line:84%
And so are we showing
full pictures?

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:14.100 line:90%
Are we sexualized?

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:16.620 line:90%
How are we showing black people?

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:20.435 line:84%
And I think that's something
that we all grapple with today.

00:03:20.435 --> 00:03:21.890 line:90%
Mm-hmm.

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:25.480 line:84%
Now, I was so into
the Carmen Jones of it

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:28.330 line:90%
all that I forgot the question.

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:29.800 line:90%
One more time.

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:31.460 line:90%
Sum it up real quick.

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:32.000 line:90%
Yes.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:36.350 line:84%
So why is it important for us
to have these conversations

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:40.610 line:84%
as Black gay men about our
masculinity, our identity,

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:45.080 line:84%
and our sexuality and amplify
our voice and visibility

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:48.867 line:84%
in Hollywood,
especially right now?

00:03:48.867 --> 00:03:50.700 line:84%
Because this is where
it starts, right here.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:54.240 line:84%
This is all where it
starts, right here.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:58.470 line:84%
There is a conception,
or a misconception,

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:04.560 line:84%
of what the African-American
gay male is.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:12.870 line:84%
And not everybody looks
like or acts like what

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:14.410 line:90%
they perceive us to be.

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:15.640 line:90%
I mean, it's a hard question.

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:16.140 line:90%
It's like--

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:17.100 line:90%
A really hard question.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:19.399 line:90%
I want to make sure I'm clear.

00:04:19.399 --> 00:04:24.660 line:84%
Also, I think a part of what I
think some of the question is--

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:28.920 line:84%
and, Lee, I'd love to hear
what you have to say about it--

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:35.070 line:84%
is how do we get
to a place where

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:38.160 line:90%
we're talking to each other.

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:40.440 line:84%
Like, so many of the
questions and so many

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:42.270 line:84%
of what we want to
talk about is always

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:46.290 line:84%
asking Hollywood to
give us something

00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:48.210 line:90%
or to make space for us.

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:52.650 line:84%
And it's like-- and it gets
harder because we're constantly

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:55.500 line:90%
begging for space to be human.

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:58.650 line:84%
Like, give us room so we
can be all the many versions

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:00.150 line:90%
of ourselves.

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:03.180 line:84%
Something that you alluded
to many times when we talked

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:05.550 line:84%
is that, look, there
is as many versions

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:09.850 line:84%
of being Black and gay as
there are Black and gay people.

00:05:09.850 --> 00:05:11.100 line:90%
There are many versions of it.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:13.620 line:84%
And if you just
show one version,

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:19.800 line:84%
then you are limiting that
people's history and humanity

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:22.970 line:90%
to that just narrow scope.

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:26.550 line:84%
And we shouldn't have to ask
to get a little bit more space

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:29.190 line:90%
to show all the variations.

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:31.050 line:84%
We shouldn't have to
keep begging for that.

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:33.300 line:84%
And so it's a hard question
to ask, because it's like,

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:39.630 line:84%
well, we've been asking
for almost 400 years.

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:47.340 line:84%
And from theater to music
to film to the internet,

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:50.430 line:84%
we keep getting these
sort of narrow channels

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:52.420 line:90%
to be fully human.

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:55.540 line:84%
And I think that's why it's
a hard question to answer.

00:05:55.540 --> 00:06:00.390 line:84%
And, Lee, you've been doing
it way longer than I have.

00:06:00.390 --> 00:06:01.432 line:90%
Thank you for that.

00:06:01.432 --> 00:06:03.390 line:84%
Thank you for that,
Tarell, because it threw me

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:05.700 line:90%
for a little bit of a loop.

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:08.160 line:84%
Yeah, I mean, it's
like peeling an onion.

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:09.600 line:90%
It's like peeling an onion.

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:13.830 line:84%
It's so layered and so
complicated, that question,

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:14.330 line:90%
Emil.

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:22.340 line:84%
It is very, very difficult
doing what we do.

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:26.430 line:84%
It is very, very
difficult having

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:30.180 line:90%
to face your own people--

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:35.680 line:84%
forget about white people,
forget about Hollywood--

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:40.300 line:84%
but your own people
as a Black gay man,

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:45.370 line:84%
as a masculine walking Black
gay man, because people

00:06:45.370 --> 00:06:47.980 line:90%
are thrown by it.

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:53.060 line:84%
They don't really see, or
they don't want to see--

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:54.420 line:90%
it confuses people.

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:56.220 line:90%
It throws people off.

00:06:56.220 --> 00:07:00.600 line:90%
And yeah.

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:10.390 line:84%
So one of the things that I want
to ask both of you is that--

00:07:10.390 --> 00:07:13.480 line:84%
within Native Son, we talk
about this in Hollywood.

00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:16.810 line:84%
We talk about it in corporate
America and in media.

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:19.240 line:90%
There is a sense of isolation.

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:22.670 line:84%
We still live in this, we're
the only one in the room.

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:24.760 line:90%
It could be at Yale.

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:31.180 line:84%
It could be at Fox or at Amazon,
wherever you're working--

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:32.380 line:90%
Netflix.

00:07:32.380 --> 00:07:33.700 line:90%
We're the only ones.

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:38.380 line:84%
And so how do you navigate
being the only one

00:07:38.380 --> 00:07:44.670 line:84%
and telling your story
without fear of being quieted?

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:46.710 line:90%
I think, for me--

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:48.270 line:90%
I can't speak for Tarell--

00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:54.280 line:84%
but for me, I grew
up in the streets.

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:57.160 line:84%
I grew up with a
family of drug dealers.

00:07:57.160 --> 00:08:03.900 line:90%
I grew up unafraid.

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:12.810 line:84%
And I took that
unafraidness into my concept

00:08:12.810 --> 00:08:15.870 line:90%
of how to break into Hollywood.

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:19.350 line:84%
No one was going to tell
me that I was nothing,

00:08:19.350 --> 00:08:21.900 line:84%
or I wasn't going to
have anybody tell me

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:24.110 line:90%
that I wasn't worthy of it.

00:08:24.110 --> 00:08:25.770 line:84%
And look, it starts
with talent first.

00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:28.930 line:90%
You have to have talent.

00:08:28.930 --> 00:08:33.240 line:90%
I think you can't just fake it.

00:08:33.240 --> 00:08:36.830 line:84%
You have to have something
that God has given you

00:08:36.830 --> 00:08:40.850 line:84%
that you know that you can
count on at the end of the day.

00:08:40.850 --> 00:08:42.740 line:90%
And once you have that--

00:08:42.740 --> 00:08:44.270 line:90%
I'm just speaking for me--

00:08:44.270 --> 00:08:51.260 line:84%
once you have what
you think is talent,

00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:57.510 line:84%
that is your backbone
for getting in the room,

00:08:57.510 --> 00:09:01.590 line:84%
as Malcolm X says, by
any means necessary.

00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:06.540 line:84%
So I didn't-- by any means
necessary is how I got

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:07.890 line:90%
into the rooms--

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:10.410 line:90%
by any means necessary.

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:13.230 line:84%
I was going to do what I had
to do to get into the room,

00:09:13.230 --> 00:09:13.890 line:90%
so that--

00:09:13.890 --> 00:09:15.900 line:84%
I was going to do what
I had to do to make sure

00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:19.500 line:84%
that Monster's Ball got into
existence, that Halle Berry won

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:24.410 line:84%
the Academy Award-- by
any means necessary.

00:09:24.410 --> 00:09:26.820 line:90%
And so now it doesn't really--

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:30.210 line:84%
it's not about by
any means necessary.

00:09:30.210 --> 00:09:32.430 line:84%
But I will resort to
that if I have to.

00:09:32.430 --> 00:09:33.330 line:90%
Right.

00:09:33.330 --> 00:09:36.360 line:84%
But do you find, now
that you're in the room,

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:40.980 line:84%
that you're still the only
Black gay man in the room who

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:43.680 line:84%
is presenting these
stories that are other,

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:47.610 line:84%
that are the story, the
narratives that we never hear?

00:09:47.610 --> 00:09:51.180 line:84%
Well, I surround myself now
with as many Black gay men

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:54.240 line:84%
as I can so I'm not the
only one in the room when

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:55.570 line:90%
I'm creating these stories.

00:09:55.570 --> 00:09:57.070 line:84%
But when I'm pitching
these stories,

00:09:57.070 --> 00:09:58.920 line:90%
oftentimes that is the case.

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:05.010 line:84%
And it is-- it's
really interesting.

00:10:05.010 --> 00:10:07.320 line:84%
This generation today,
we're in a different space

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:12.030 line:84%
where people just are
accepting anything

00:10:12.030 --> 00:10:15.780 line:84%
as talent because it's
other, as opposed to looking

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:18.300 line:90%
at the talent now.

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:23.640 line:84%
Now, the message is woke,
as opposed to the talent.

00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:24.630 line:90%
Is it woke?

00:10:24.630 --> 00:10:26.610 line:90%
OK, let's do it.

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:29.080 line:90%
Let's do it.

00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:31.830 line:84%
So a lot of people are
getting passes on mediocrity--

00:10:34.930 --> 00:10:37.600 line:90%
gay people and Black people.

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:40.750 line:84%
But no, you have to
be born with a talent.

00:10:40.750 --> 00:10:41.970 line:90%
You have to be--

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:45.477 line:84%
it's a gift from God that
that drives you forward

00:10:45.477 --> 00:10:46.060 line:90%
into the room.

00:10:46.060 --> 00:10:51.680 line:84%
It propels you into
a space where that--

00:10:51.680 --> 00:10:53.490 line:84%
and I can only,
again, speak for me--

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:55.590 line:84%
where you do not take
no for an answer.

00:10:55.590 --> 00:10:57.820 line:84%
And this is going
to happen my way.

00:10:57.820 --> 00:10:58.620 line:90%
Right.

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:02.040 line:90%
So, Tarell, what about you?

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:04.230 line:84%
Being the only one
in the room, how

00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:08.340 line:84%
do you navigate that
from a creative space,

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:15.450 line:84%
from an empowering space,
from a survival space?

00:11:15.450 --> 00:11:19.350 line:84%
Well, first of all, I think
white people have been allowed

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:21.690 line:90%
mediocrity for 400 years.

00:11:21.690 --> 00:11:27.150 line:84%
And I think-- so I think I'm all
right with a little mediocrity

00:11:27.150 --> 00:11:29.080 line:90%
here and there from us.

00:11:29.080 --> 00:11:31.500 line:84%
We've had to be super
and over-talented

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:33.380 line:90%
in every way, shape, or form.

00:11:33.380 --> 00:11:39.390 line:84%
And so I think I
don't mind so much.

00:11:39.390 --> 00:11:41.630 line:90%
I also am very much--

00:11:41.630 --> 00:11:44.130 line:84%
like Lee said, I
make sure when I

00:11:44.130 --> 00:11:49.860 line:84%
do say I'm blessed and
lucky and fortunate

00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:52.980 line:84%
and had people like Lee
and like many people

00:11:52.980 --> 00:11:55.980 line:84%
before me to make
spaces for me and inform

00:11:55.980 --> 00:11:57.730 line:90%
the stories I want to tell.

00:11:57.730 --> 00:12:04.560 line:84%
And in that way,
I'm able to say, no,

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:08.880 line:84%
you can't fill my room with
as many white executives

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:09.510 line:90%
as you want.

00:12:09.510 --> 00:12:12.570 line:84%
I actually would like
for you to fill it with--

00:12:12.570 --> 00:12:13.508 line:90%
it's almost as if--

00:12:13.508 --> 00:12:15.300 line:84%
and it was so funny,
because my best friend

00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:17.440 line:84%
and I were having
this conversation.

00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:18.480 line:90%
I really wanted to do--

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:20.820 line:84%
I wanted to work
with some writers,

00:12:20.820 --> 00:12:25.170 line:84%
some Black gay writers,
who we respect.

00:12:25.170 --> 00:12:26.320 line:90%
We're around the same age.

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:29.010 line:90%
We respect each other's work.

00:12:29.010 --> 00:12:32.490 line:84%
And when I said, hey, we all
want to do a project together,

00:12:32.490 --> 00:12:35.370 line:84%
people looked at me
like I was crazy,

00:12:35.370 --> 00:12:37.140 line:84%
because you're just
not allowed to put

00:12:37.140 --> 00:12:41.160 line:84%
that many of the same thing
in the room when it's black.

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:42.150 line:90%
You can't put that--

00:12:42.150 --> 00:12:43.510 line:84%
you're going to put all
of them in the same room

00:12:43.510 --> 00:12:44.880 line:90%
and let them do something?

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:45.750 line:90%
That's wild.

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:50.460 line:84%
But as Lee said,
you sometimes just

00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:52.170 line:84%
have to keep saying
no until the room

00:12:52.170 --> 00:12:54.630 line:90%
looks more and more like you.

00:12:54.630 --> 00:13:00.090 line:84%
You keep saying no to
all of the other reasons

00:13:00.090 --> 00:13:04.140 line:84%
why you should go with this
tried and true method that--

00:13:04.140 --> 00:13:12.090 line:84%
rather than the unpaved ground,
the ground that's untrodden.

00:13:12.090 --> 00:13:14.820 line:84%
And so I deal with
it in that way.

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:18.230 line:84%
And unlikely, I'm not
as brave all the time.

00:13:18.230 --> 00:13:20.005 line:90%
You know, I do get scared.

00:13:20.005 --> 00:13:21.630 line:84%
And I get scared
because I want to make

00:13:21.630 --> 00:13:24.210 line:90%
sure I'm protecting folk.

00:13:24.210 --> 00:13:25.620 line:90%
People are vicious.

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:28.080 line:90%
People are vicious and mean.

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:30.510 line:84%
And I don't want to
bring anybody up and not

00:13:30.510 --> 00:13:31.890 line:90%
be able to protect them.

00:13:31.890 --> 00:13:33.360 line:90%
And so you just look around.

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:36.090 line:84%
You're just bringing people to
a place, and they're not ready

00:13:36.090 --> 00:13:38.340 line:84%
and not looking out
for all the things

00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:44.280 line:84%
that this very capitalist,
very hungry industry can do.

00:13:44.280 --> 00:13:50.530 line:84%
You sometimes watch a friend
get really hurt by other stuff.

00:13:50.530 --> 00:13:52.380 line:84%
And I think, you
know, anything--

00:13:52.380 --> 00:13:54.180 line:84%
bell hooks has a
saying about anything

00:13:54.180 --> 00:14:01.350 line:84%
that estranges us or
alienates us one from another,

00:14:01.350 --> 00:14:04.645 line:84%
it serves the interests of
racism and discrimination.

00:14:04.645 --> 00:14:05.145 line:90%
And I just--

00:14:08.370 --> 00:14:09.890 line:90%
I'm a protector in that way.

00:14:09.890 --> 00:14:12.570 line:84%
So I try my best to make sure,
hey, if I'm bringing you along,

00:14:12.570 --> 00:14:14.850 line:84%
I want to make sure that
we're in this together,

00:14:14.850 --> 00:14:17.400 line:84%
and that I have enough
energy and strength

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:21.420 line:84%
and focus that I can make sure
that we're all doing our best

00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:28.765 line:90%
work here on sort of just--

00:14:31.630 --> 00:14:33.340 line:90%
as free as we can be.

00:14:33.340 --> 00:14:34.840 line:90%
I was about to say free.

00:14:34.840 --> 00:14:39.770 line:84%
But you know, in America,
there's such a tax on that word

00:14:39.770 --> 00:14:40.270 line:90%
now.

00:14:40.270 --> 00:14:42.103 line:84%
We don't even know if
we can use it anymore.

00:14:42.103 --> 00:14:44.300 line:90%
Are we really free?

00:14:44.300 --> 00:14:45.550 line:90%
But we're trying to get there.

00:14:45.550 --> 00:14:46.860 line:90%
I'm trying to get free.

00:14:46.860 --> 00:14:48.580 line:84%
Tarell, that's why
I love you, man.

00:14:48.580 --> 00:14:50.530 line:90%
I mean, the idea--

00:14:50.530 --> 00:14:53.450 line:84%
and it's a total
generational thing.

00:14:53.450 --> 00:14:56.560 line:84%
Like, I would have never thought
to think of the mediocrity

00:14:56.560 --> 00:14:57.880 line:90%
that white people have put out.

00:14:57.880 --> 00:14:58.900 line:90%
This is the--

00:14:58.900 --> 00:15:01.400 line:84%
I mean, of course,
it's a no-brainer.

00:15:01.400 --> 00:15:02.470 line:90%
It is a no-brainer.

00:15:02.470 --> 00:15:07.410 line:84%
But we have been
trained, brainwashed

00:15:07.410 --> 00:15:13.200 line:84%
to believe that we have
to be the best that we

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:15.570 line:90%
are in whatever it is.

00:15:15.570 --> 00:15:19.600 line:84%
If they are good, we
have to be even better.

00:15:19.600 --> 00:15:22.590 line:84%
So already that comment right
there shows the disconnect

00:15:22.590 --> 00:15:26.580 line:84%
from my generation
to your generation,

00:15:26.580 --> 00:15:29.810 line:84%
and thank you for
correcting me on that.

00:15:29.810 --> 00:15:31.100 line:90%
And that's good.

00:15:31.100 --> 00:15:33.770 line:84%
It's part of-- no,
it's an evolution.

00:15:33.770 --> 00:15:39.920 line:84%
I am learning so
much from this change

00:15:39.920 --> 00:15:41.900 line:90%
that's happening right now.

00:15:41.900 --> 00:15:46.460 line:84%
Because I approached
the rooms, I

00:15:46.460 --> 00:15:52.310 line:84%
approached getting projects
done in such a iconoclastic way.

00:15:52.310 --> 00:15:58.190 line:84%
It just wasn't-- it was
by any means necessary.

00:15:58.190 --> 00:16:03.210 line:84%
And then when I was on, I
had to make sure that once it

00:16:03.210 --> 00:16:06.405 line:90%
was done that it was perfect.

00:16:10.250 --> 00:16:14.000 line:84%
So I won't be so
hard, I guess, on some

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:16.580 line:84%
of the African-American-- some
of the Black stuff that I've

00:16:16.580 --> 00:16:17.450 line:90%
seen.

00:16:17.450 --> 00:16:18.920 line:90%
I'm hard on it.

00:16:18.920 --> 00:16:22.130 line:84%
I'm hard on stuff,
anyway, but I'm extra hard

00:16:22.130 --> 00:16:23.760 line:90%
on what it is that we have.

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:24.720 line:90%
And I shouldn't be.

00:16:24.720 --> 00:16:25.470 line:90%
This is a wake-up.

00:16:25.470 --> 00:16:26.580 line:90%
Thank you for that.

00:16:26.580 --> 00:16:30.920 line:84%
No, I think-- listen, I believe
we must make space so that we

00:16:30.920 --> 00:16:35.040 line:84%
can interrogate, we
can critique, we can--

00:16:35.040 --> 00:16:38.288 line:84%
but we need to do that together
and have those conversations.

00:16:38.288 --> 00:16:39.080 line:90%
That's how we grow.

00:16:39.080 --> 00:16:41.300 line:90%
That's how cultures grow.

00:16:41.300 --> 00:16:46.220 line:90%
Nothing gets a "pass."

00:16:46.220 --> 00:16:49.010 line:84%
Especially with us, we
can go to the barbershop,

00:16:49.010 --> 00:16:52.760 line:84%
and he going to have
five or six variations

00:16:52.760 --> 00:16:57.290 line:90%
on why that film didn't work.

00:16:57.290 --> 00:16:59.778 line:84%
I mean, I've had people sit
me down and tell me, ain't

00:16:59.778 --> 00:17:01.070 line:90%
seen a play ever in their life.

00:17:01.070 --> 00:17:03.195 line:84%
I was the first play they
ever went to on Broadway.

00:17:03.195 --> 00:17:06.200 line:84%
And then telling me, well,
you know, in that third act,

00:17:06.200 --> 00:17:08.150 line:90%
if you would've just did x.

00:17:08.150 --> 00:17:11.260 line:90%
And you know-- and that's OK.

00:17:11.260 --> 00:17:12.560 line:90%
I think there's moments.

00:17:12.560 --> 00:17:15.380 line:84%
But I think in terms of-- or
at least the way the question

00:17:15.380 --> 00:17:22.550 line:84%
was placed to me, in terms of
trying to live up to whatever

00:17:22.550 --> 00:17:25.790 line:84%
they need us to live up to
or trying to push something

00:17:25.790 --> 00:17:28.280 line:84%
uphill, it's like, yo,
if they highlight--

00:17:28.280 --> 00:17:32.600 line:84%
if they greenlighting stuff
that they don't know is mediocre

00:17:32.600 --> 00:17:35.600 line:84%
and there's a brother or a
sister getting paid for it,

00:17:35.600 --> 00:17:38.490 line:84%
I'm not about to
block anybody's money.

00:17:38.490 --> 00:17:40.070 line:90%
It's too rough out here.

00:17:40.070 --> 00:17:43.760 line:84%
But does that mean I don't
want to help and encourage

00:17:43.760 --> 00:17:48.050 line:84%
rigor and exploration of their
best art and their best form?

00:17:48.050 --> 00:17:49.880 line:90%
Of course, but that's for us.

00:17:49.880 --> 00:17:52.080 line:84%
I want you to do your
best not because I want

00:17:52.080 --> 00:17:53.330 line:90%
you to prove anything to them.

00:17:53.330 --> 00:17:54.872 line:84%
I want you to do
your best because it

00:17:54.872 --> 00:17:58.500 line:84%
will help us look at ourselves
and see who we are better.

00:17:58.500 --> 00:17:59.540 line:90%
Yeah.

00:17:59.540 --> 00:18:00.500 line:90%
It's interesting.

00:18:00.500 --> 00:18:05.870 line:84%
You mentioned, Tarell,
how gracious Lee was

00:18:05.870 --> 00:18:10.058 line:84%
and how he really sat you
down and talked to you.

00:18:10.058 --> 00:18:11.600 line:84%
And so it's funny,
Lee, to hear you--

00:18:11.600 --> 00:18:14.990 line:84%
Well, Lee doesn't sit
anybody down to talk to him.

00:18:14.990 --> 00:18:17.520 line:90%
[LAUGHS] Just rolls up on them.

00:18:17.520 --> 00:18:19.240 line:90%
Lee going to tell you on the--

00:18:19.240 --> 00:18:21.020 line:90%
Lee was like, come to set.

00:18:21.020 --> 00:18:23.030 line:84%
And while I'm doing
70 different things,

00:18:23.030 --> 00:18:24.680 line:90%
I'ma drop some gems on you.

00:18:24.680 --> 00:18:27.930 line:84%
So I don't know if there was
a sit-down, as opposed to,

00:18:27.930 --> 00:18:30.380 line:84%
you see that, don't do
that shit, moving on.

00:18:30.380 --> 00:18:31.460 line:90%
Right.

00:18:31.460 --> 00:18:34.910 line:90%
I get those calls all the time.

00:18:34.910 --> 00:18:37.460 line:84%
What were some of
the things that Lee

00:18:37.460 --> 00:18:39.830 line:84%
dropped on you and wisdom
that he shared with you

00:18:39.830 --> 00:18:44.110 line:90%
that helped you pave your way?

00:18:44.110 --> 00:18:47.458 line:84%
I just remember there was
one thing that Lee said--

00:18:47.458 --> 00:18:49.000 line:84%
there were many
things that Lee said.

00:18:49.000 --> 00:18:51.208 line:84%
But there was one thing that
Lee said that I actually

00:18:51.208 --> 00:18:54.250 line:84%
say to my students and
to myself consistently,

00:18:54.250 --> 00:18:57.220 line:84%
like a little mantra, which
is that they don't know us

00:18:57.220 --> 00:18:58.870 line:90%
better than we know us.

00:18:58.870 --> 00:19:02.690 line:84%
Don't ever let them make you
pretend or pay you enough money

00:19:02.690 --> 00:19:05.980 line:84%
to get you to say that
they actually know more

00:19:05.980 --> 00:19:07.330 line:90%
about us than they do.

00:19:07.330 --> 00:19:11.110 line:84%
You don't have-- and just like
I said about the nurse working

00:19:11.110 --> 00:19:15.550 line:84%
five shifts knows more
about Halle Berry's

00:19:15.550 --> 00:19:19.210 line:84%
struggle in Monster's Ball
than any executive does

00:19:19.210 --> 00:19:20.260 line:90%
and that she knows it.

00:19:20.260 --> 00:19:21.593 line:90%
She knows what that struggle is.

00:19:21.593 --> 00:19:25.330 line:84%
She knows what
that-- and so don't--

00:19:25.330 --> 00:19:26.225 line:90%
trust that.

00:19:26.225 --> 00:19:27.100 line:90%
You have to trust it.

00:19:27.100 --> 00:19:32.950 line:84%
You have to trust that you know
this life, this world, this--

00:19:32.950 --> 00:19:34.900 line:84%
even if it's fantasy,
even if it's sci-fi,

00:19:34.900 --> 00:19:39.520 line:84%
from this perspective you know
it, and you hold the key to it.

00:19:39.520 --> 00:19:42.700 line:84%
And it just helps me, because
there have been many a moment

00:19:42.700 --> 00:19:44.530 line:84%
where someone has
asked me to explain

00:19:44.530 --> 00:19:47.650 line:84%
my Blackness or my queerness
or my Black queerness.

00:19:47.650 --> 00:19:50.520 line:84%
And I've just been
like, I'm good today.

00:19:50.520 --> 00:19:52.210 line:84%
Like, I'm good on
explaining it today.

00:19:52.210 --> 00:19:54.070 line:84%
Like, you either
get it or you don't.

00:19:54.070 --> 00:19:57.280 line:90%
Or don't pay for it.

00:19:57.280 --> 00:19:58.920 line:90%
Don't greenlight it.

00:19:58.920 --> 00:20:01.210 line:90%
But I'm not explaining it today.

00:20:01.210 --> 00:20:06.790 line:84%
And I think I definitely
owe that to Lee.

00:20:06.790 --> 00:20:11.170 line:84%
Like, I hear him saying it in my
mind when those moments happen.

00:20:11.170 --> 00:20:13.032 line:90%
Thanks, Tarell.

00:20:13.032 --> 00:20:14.240 line:90%
Yeah, it's interesting, Lee--

00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:15.880 line:90%
Thank you.

00:20:15.880 --> 00:20:16.520 line:90%
Right.

00:20:16.520 --> 00:20:18.920 line:90%
We thank you, Lee, for sure.

00:20:18.920 --> 00:20:22.850 line:84%
It's interesting, Lee, that
you don't think that you are--

00:20:22.850 --> 00:20:25.370 line:84%
that you're not kind of
moving things forward

00:20:25.370 --> 00:20:26.270 line:90%
in a radical way.

00:20:26.270 --> 00:20:29.750 line:84%
I think that every
story that you've told

00:20:29.750 --> 00:20:34.790 line:84%
and every way that you
fundraised and cast,

00:20:34.790 --> 00:20:36.980 line:84%
you've always pushed
outside of the box

00:20:36.980 --> 00:20:41.480 line:84%
to create opportunities for
other Black gay storytellers

00:20:41.480 --> 00:20:43.490 line:84%
to come up, for
someone like Tarell

00:20:43.490 --> 00:20:46.820 line:84%
to be able to win the
Oscar for Moonlight

00:20:46.820 --> 00:20:50.840 line:84%
and for Jordan E. Cooper
to have an opportunity

00:20:50.840 --> 00:20:52.370 line:90%
to do storytelling.

00:20:55.100 --> 00:21:00.380 line:84%
Well, it comes second nature,
because the gift is not yours.

00:21:00.380 --> 00:21:02.760 line:90%
God didn't give you the gift.

00:21:02.760 --> 00:21:04.980 line:90%
The gift is that.

00:21:04.980 --> 00:21:08.820 line:84%
So your gift is to make
sure that it's passed on.

00:21:08.820 --> 00:21:11.430 line:84%
And you know, there was
a time in Hollywood,

00:21:11.430 --> 00:21:14.390 line:84%
when I came to Hollywood, where
you had to be the only one.

00:21:14.390 --> 00:21:15.390 line:90%
It was very competitive.

00:21:15.390 --> 00:21:19.110 line:84%
And I mean, I was the
only Black gay person that

00:21:19.110 --> 00:21:23.720 line:84%
was out at least, that
was out as a filmmaker.

00:21:23.720 --> 00:21:29.930 line:84%
But there was major competition
and the crabs in the barrel

00:21:29.930 --> 00:21:32.570 line:90%
thing amongst Black people.

00:21:35.960 --> 00:21:38.510 line:84%
And so you didn't
really pass it on.

00:21:38.510 --> 00:21:39.320 line:90%
It was all BS.

00:21:39.320 --> 00:21:41.570 line:90%
You really didn't pass it on.

00:21:41.570 --> 00:21:45.620 line:84%
But I knew instinctively,
as a gay man,

00:21:45.620 --> 00:21:48.350 line:84%
that I had to pass it
on, because otherwise I

00:21:48.350 --> 00:21:50.090 line:90%
would be the only one.

00:21:50.090 --> 00:21:51.170 line:90%
And I needed that to--

00:21:51.170 --> 00:21:51.920 line:90%
I needed that.

00:21:51.920 --> 00:21:53.337 line:84%
I knew that there
were people that

00:21:53.337 --> 00:21:55.613 line:84%
were far more talented
than me that were out there

00:21:55.613 --> 00:21:56.530 line:90%
that I really wanted--

00:21:56.530 --> 00:21:59.870 line:84%
far more talented than me that
I wanted to hear their voices.

00:21:59.870 --> 00:22:01.590 line:84%
And I wanted to
see that direction.

00:22:01.590 --> 00:22:03.350 line:84%
And I wanted to see
their productions.

00:22:03.350 --> 00:22:07.400 line:84%
And so if I didn't pass
it on, I was missing out

00:22:07.400 --> 00:22:10.440 line:90%
on the opportunity.

00:22:10.440 --> 00:22:12.020 line:90%
Does that make any sense?

00:22:12.020 --> 00:22:14.060 line:90%
Yeah, absolutely.

00:22:14.060 --> 00:22:14.930 line:90%
Absolutely.

00:22:14.930 --> 00:22:15.510 line:90%
How do we--

00:22:15.510 --> 00:22:16.570 line:90%
So I don't really look at it--

00:22:16.570 --> 00:22:17.070 line:90%
I'm sorry.

00:22:17.070 --> 00:22:20.300 line:90%
I don't really look at it as a--

00:22:20.300 --> 00:22:22.620 line:90%
for me it was survival.

00:22:22.620 --> 00:22:25.940 line:90%
It was about-- it is about--

00:22:25.940 --> 00:22:28.520 line:84%
it's never been about the money,
ever been about the money.

00:22:28.520 --> 00:22:33.500 line:84%
It is about making sure
that your work lives forever

00:22:33.500 --> 00:22:39.590 line:84%
and that I am affecting, in
particular, young Black kids

00:22:39.590 --> 00:22:42.200 line:90%
that are gay, in particular.

00:22:42.200 --> 00:22:44.990 line:84%
What message am I
sending to them?

00:22:44.990 --> 00:22:48.560 line:90%
That's what it's about.

00:22:48.560 --> 00:22:50.895 line:84%
Even if the situations
are provocative,

00:22:50.895 --> 00:22:53.020 line:84%
or if it's a sexual situation,
or if it's something

00:22:53.020 --> 00:22:53.770 line:90%
that they should--

00:22:53.770 --> 00:22:59.530 line:84%
like, what is it that will
make these kids think that they

00:22:59.530 --> 00:23:00.100 line:90%
also--

00:23:00.100 --> 00:23:02.320 line:84%
wow, this is a
world that I can see

00:23:02.320 --> 00:23:08.050 line:90%
that I know that we don't see.

00:23:08.050 --> 00:23:11.320 line:84%
What is Lee bringing to
us that we don't see?

00:23:11.320 --> 00:23:13.930 line:90%
And, Tarell, how do you--

00:23:13.930 --> 00:23:14.990 line:90%
And that's very scary.

00:23:14.990 --> 00:23:17.260 line:90%
That's scary, Emil, to do that.

00:23:17.260 --> 00:23:19.930 line:84%
Not to cut you off, but
that's really scary.

00:23:19.930 --> 00:23:22.540 line:84%
When you're in the middle
of that, I am afraid.

00:23:22.540 --> 00:23:23.770 line:90%
I'm not afraid of getting it.

00:23:23.770 --> 00:23:27.710 line:90%
Or I'm not afraid of--

00:23:27.710 --> 00:23:32.220 line:84%
I am not afraid of
getting the money

00:23:32.220 --> 00:23:34.420 line:84%
or convincing somebody
else to tell the story.

00:23:34.420 --> 00:23:37.350 line:84%
But once you're in the
middle of telling the story,

00:23:37.350 --> 00:23:39.240 line:90%
I am terrified.

00:23:39.240 --> 00:23:45.900 line:84%
And are you terrified because
you want it to be authentic,

00:23:45.900 --> 00:23:49.330 line:90%
that you want it to be healing?

00:23:49.330 --> 00:23:50.070 line:90%
I mean, what is--

00:23:50.070 --> 00:23:51.660 line:90%
All of the above.

00:23:51.660 --> 00:23:54.650 line:90%
Am I healing someone?

00:23:54.650 --> 00:23:59.290 line:84%
It's as close to
God as I get, when

00:23:59.290 --> 00:24:01.810 line:90%
I'm in that space of creating.

00:24:01.810 --> 00:24:06.190 line:84%
So I want to make sure that it
is definitely affecting you.

00:24:06.190 --> 00:24:12.690 line:84%
And if I'm in the face of God,
I am happy and I am afraid.

00:24:12.690 --> 00:24:17.250 line:84%
And then that fear then
drives the creativity as well?

00:24:17.250 --> 00:24:18.600 line:90%
Yes.

00:24:18.600 --> 00:24:19.100 line:90%
Sorry.

00:24:19.100 --> 00:24:20.530 line:90%
Go back to Tarell.

00:24:20.530 --> 00:24:23.840 line:84%
No, no, this is
the conversation.

00:24:23.840 --> 00:24:28.080 line:90%
So there's no start and stop.

00:24:28.080 --> 00:24:31.610 line:84%
Tarell, you talked about
bringing other Black talent

00:24:31.610 --> 00:24:33.080 line:90%
along.

00:24:33.080 --> 00:24:35.670 line:90%
How do you do that?

00:24:35.670 --> 00:24:39.890 line:84%
How do you do that through
your production deals?

00:24:39.890 --> 00:24:42.380 line:84%
How do you do that
as you're navigating

00:24:42.380 --> 00:24:46.490 line:90%
your own path in Hollywood?

00:24:46.490 --> 00:24:48.740 line:90%
I mean, there's no formula.

00:24:48.740 --> 00:24:50.180 line:90%
I think I just look around.

00:24:50.180 --> 00:24:53.320 line:84%
And every project
that I'm working on,

00:24:53.320 --> 00:24:56.690 line:84%
I try to make sure
that Black is--

00:24:56.690 --> 00:24:58.280 line:84%
that the people who
are working on it

00:24:58.280 --> 00:24:59.855 line:90%
are as Black as the story.

00:25:02.810 --> 00:25:06.350 line:90%
I mean, it's true.

00:25:06.350 --> 00:25:07.850 line:90%
I try to.

00:25:07.850 --> 00:25:11.810 line:84%
And sometimes I
don't get it 100%.

00:25:11.810 --> 00:25:18.290 line:84%
Sometimes the director might not
be Black, or some of the cast

00:25:18.290 --> 00:25:20.870 line:84%
might not be Black or some
of the grip or the DP.

00:25:20.870 --> 00:25:21.530 line:90%
But I try.

00:25:21.530 --> 00:25:25.040 line:84%
I really do try to
just go, oh, yo,

00:25:25.040 --> 00:25:26.750 line:84%
artist who's been
working in Chicago

00:25:26.750 --> 00:25:28.880 line:90%
and killing it for 20 years.

00:25:28.880 --> 00:25:30.980 line:84%
You've never had
a role like this.

00:25:30.980 --> 00:25:32.510 line:90%
Here, please take it.

00:25:32.510 --> 00:25:36.260 line:84%
And have to spend the
kind of time and effort

00:25:36.260 --> 00:25:38.990 line:84%
and making sure
people understand why

00:25:38.990 --> 00:25:42.170 line:84%
that this is such a pivotal
point for this artist.

00:25:42.170 --> 00:25:44.630 line:84%
But I just keep
pulling where I can.

00:25:47.270 --> 00:25:50.040 line:84%
And I do that pretty
unapologetically.

00:25:50.040 --> 00:25:52.860 line:84%
Now, like I said,
I'm not perfect,

00:25:52.860 --> 00:25:54.680 line:90%
and I definitely tire out.

00:25:54.680 --> 00:25:55.760 line:90%
So sometimes I fail.

00:25:55.760 --> 00:25:58.160 line:84%
Sometimes I don't
get it to the--

00:25:58.160 --> 00:26:00.860 line:84%
but I look to people
like Lee, to like

00:26:00.860 --> 00:26:04.340 line:84%
Ava, who figure out
ways to go, oh, no,

00:26:04.340 --> 00:26:08.450 line:84%
every director on this episode
is going to be a Black woman.

00:26:08.450 --> 00:26:14.260 line:84%
Or every DP or every camera
person is going to be x.

00:26:14.260 --> 00:26:16.490 line:84%
I try to get that
organizational voice.

00:26:16.490 --> 00:26:18.680 line:84%
But as Lee said, sometimes
when I get mired down

00:26:18.680 --> 00:26:20.420 line:84%
in the writing of
the thing, I just

00:26:20.420 --> 00:26:23.240 line:84%
get so focused on making sure
that the word is right that I

00:26:23.240 --> 00:26:24.560 line:90%
look up and I go, damn it.

00:26:24.560 --> 00:26:28.640 line:84%
I also need to pay attention
to these seven other things.

00:26:28.640 --> 00:26:30.920 line:90%
But if you don't--

00:26:30.920 --> 00:26:32.690 line:90%
for me it's also--

00:26:32.690 --> 00:26:37.310 line:84%
I need community,
for me personally.

00:26:37.310 --> 00:26:38.090 line:90%
I need to look--

00:26:38.090 --> 00:26:39.965 line:84%
I need to be able to
look around and go, hey,

00:26:39.965 --> 00:26:42.800 line:84%
there's [INAUDIBLE] to
someone who understands that

00:26:42.800 --> 00:26:44.060 line:90%
without having to explain it.

00:26:44.060 --> 00:26:49.730 line:84%
So I just try to move
with rather than solo.

00:26:49.730 --> 00:26:51.780 line:84%
And there's tempting
moments all the time

00:26:51.780 --> 00:26:54.110 line:84%
where people will go, oh,
come on through this door.

00:26:54.110 --> 00:26:56.735 line:84%
And then you try to come in, and
all your people come with you.

00:26:56.735 --> 00:26:59.510 line:84%
And they're like, oh,
not all y'all, just you.

00:26:59.510 --> 00:27:02.790 line:84%
And you kind of have to be
like, oh, well, we can't all--

00:27:02.790 --> 00:27:06.380 line:84%
I mean, I need them
in order to sit down.

00:27:06.380 --> 00:27:08.330 line:84%
I need them in
order to stay sane.

00:27:08.330 --> 00:27:11.360 line:84%
I need them in order not to
throttle you in five seconds

00:27:11.360 --> 00:27:13.360 line:90%
because they keep me easy.

00:27:13.360 --> 00:27:17.300 line:84%
And so you kind of have
to move as one sometimes.

00:27:17.300 --> 00:27:21.530 line:84%
And I know-- and
the saying is often,

00:27:21.530 --> 00:27:23.280 line:84%
like, oh, well, I'll
get through the door,

00:27:23.280 --> 00:27:24.613 line:90%
and then I'll open some for you.

00:27:24.613 --> 00:27:27.500 line:84%
It's like, well, sometimes
I just will stay outside

00:27:27.500 --> 00:27:29.055 line:90%
until we all can get in.

00:27:29.055 --> 00:27:30.680 line:84%
And I know people
have been like, well,

00:27:30.680 --> 00:27:33.410 line:84%
that's why you aren't
part of the Academy.

00:27:33.410 --> 00:27:34.910 line:84%
That's why you
aren't this and that.

00:27:34.910 --> 00:27:37.100 line:90%
I'm like, that's cool.

00:27:37.100 --> 00:27:38.550 line:90%
That's fine.

00:27:38.550 --> 00:27:39.770 line:90%
But I'll wait.

00:27:39.770 --> 00:27:45.280 line:84%
I'd rather wait till we all
can kind of walk on through.

00:27:45.280 --> 00:27:48.130 line:84%
So you're not apart
of the Academy,

00:27:48.130 --> 00:27:54.490 line:84%
but you have won an Oscar for
your writing for Moonlight.

00:27:54.490 --> 00:27:58.360 line:90%
This is so upsetting to even--

00:27:58.360 --> 00:27:59.860 line:90%
we talked about this earlier.

00:27:59.860 --> 00:28:03.040 line:90%
And it traumatized me.

00:28:03.040 --> 00:28:06.370 line:84%
That little rehearsal we
had, that conversation

00:28:06.370 --> 00:28:10.510 line:84%
that we had earlier when I got
that information spun me out

00:28:10.510 --> 00:28:13.290 line:90%
of control.

00:28:13.290 --> 00:28:19.120 line:84%
And it's indicative of the space
that we are in at the Academy.

00:28:19.120 --> 00:28:20.050 line:90%
We're trying.

00:28:20.050 --> 00:28:23.667 line:84%
Again, I bring it up because
that was a genuine conversation

00:28:23.667 --> 00:28:24.250 line:90%
that happened.

00:28:24.250 --> 00:28:27.460 line:84%
Someone literally said to
me, well, you need to fight.

00:28:27.460 --> 00:28:30.053 line:84%
You need to fight for your
position and your standing.

00:28:30.053 --> 00:28:31.720 line:84%
And I was like, well,
I'm sure there are

00:28:31.720 --> 00:28:34.070 line:90%
other people who didn't get--

00:28:34.070 --> 00:28:37.120 line:84%
I'm sure there's a list
of folks who didn't

00:28:37.120 --> 00:28:38.650 line:90%
walk through the threshold.

00:28:38.650 --> 00:28:45.190 line:84%
And I'd rather fight for
us than just my spot.

00:28:45.190 --> 00:28:46.360 line:90%
And what would it--

00:28:46.360 --> 00:28:47.868 line:84%
and again, it's
that thing about--

00:28:47.868 --> 00:28:50.410 line:84%
But, Tarell, let me just tell
you, you will be in the Academy

00:28:50.410 --> 00:28:51.250 line:90%
this year.

00:28:51.250 --> 00:28:52.990 line:90%
My God.

00:28:52.990 --> 00:28:57.180 line:84%
So that's a-- this is
a wrap conversation.

00:28:57.180 --> 00:28:58.360 line:90%
That's done.

00:28:58.360 --> 00:28:59.520 line:90%
Speaking of, let me just--

00:28:59.520 --> 00:29:01.560 line:84%
I'm going to talk about
your work for a second.

00:29:01.560 --> 00:29:02.970 line:90%
Wig Out!

00:29:08.460 --> 00:29:09.627 line:90%
You do feel alone.

00:29:09.627 --> 00:29:11.460 line:84%
Emil, I don't know
whether you saw the play.

00:29:11.460 --> 00:29:12.680 line:90%
Did you see the play?

00:29:12.680 --> 00:29:13.530 line:90%
I saw it.

00:29:13.530 --> 00:29:14.280 line:90%
[INAUDIBLE]

00:29:14.280 --> 00:29:17.590 line:84%
So I always feel
that I am alone.

00:29:17.590 --> 00:29:20.760 line:84%
I felt that way until
I saw this cat's work.

00:29:20.760 --> 00:29:21.870 line:90%
You just feel like--

00:29:24.870 --> 00:29:29.720 line:84%
the specificity of that
Black gay man's mind

00:29:29.720 --> 00:29:31.440 line:90%
is-- it's so specific.

00:29:31.440 --> 00:29:37.470 line:84%
It's just so-- the humor,
the banter, the pacing,

00:29:37.470 --> 00:29:38.850 line:90%
the everything.

00:29:38.850 --> 00:29:41.838 line:84%
I sat there and
watched that play.

00:29:41.838 --> 00:29:42.630 line:90%
I don't know where.

00:29:42.630 --> 00:29:43.380 line:90%
Was it the public?

00:29:43.380 --> 00:29:44.072 line:90%
Where was it at?

00:29:44.072 --> 00:29:45.280 line:90%
I don't know where it was at.

00:29:45.280 --> 00:29:45.960 line:90%
But it was--

00:29:45.960 --> 00:29:47.310 line:90%
It was at the venue.

00:29:47.310 --> 00:29:51.120 line:90%
Dude, let me tell you.

00:29:51.120 --> 00:29:53.100 line:90%
I saw the birth of a soul.

00:29:53.100 --> 00:29:55.770 line:90%
And I knew that--

00:29:55.770 --> 00:29:56.880 line:90%
it was a given.

00:29:56.880 --> 00:30:00.780 line:84%
And it made me feel so
good that I wasn't alone.

00:30:00.780 --> 00:30:06.450 line:84%
I've felt alone-- well,
George Wolfe's earlier works--

00:30:06.450 --> 00:30:12.330 line:84%
Colored Museum-- I felt
like, OK, he's out there.

00:30:12.330 --> 00:30:13.710 line:90%
But George was out there.

00:30:17.060 --> 00:30:18.170 line:90%
And then I saw this cat.

00:30:18.170 --> 00:30:21.550 line:84%
It was like, oh, man,
you're not alone.

00:30:21.550 --> 00:30:24.320 line:84%
And it's a really great
feeling to not be alone.

00:30:26.910 --> 00:30:27.530 line:90%
It's good.

00:30:27.530 --> 00:30:29.140 line:90%
And I'm sure that there--

00:30:29.140 --> 00:30:32.900 line:90%
Jordan Cooper's another.

00:30:32.900 --> 00:30:34.640 line:90%
Julian Breece is another.

00:30:39.850 --> 00:30:41.510 line:90%
It's what we have gone through.

00:30:41.510 --> 00:30:45.400 line:84%
It's what we've experienced
as Black gay men.

00:30:45.400 --> 00:30:47.020 line:90%
It's very specific, the feeling.

00:30:49.970 --> 00:30:54.000 line:84%
And when you are able to
express that in any form,

00:30:54.000 --> 00:30:56.960 line:90%
it's pretty breathtaking.

00:30:56.960 --> 00:31:00.270 line:90%
It's pretty groundbreaking.

00:31:00.270 --> 00:31:02.140 line:90%
It's what we bring to the table.

00:31:02.140 --> 00:31:03.540 line:90%
Yup.

00:31:03.540 --> 00:31:05.730 line:90%
Yup!

00:31:05.730 --> 00:31:10.440 line:84%
So how do we bring more
people to the table?

00:31:10.440 --> 00:31:12.418 line:84%
I mean, I understand
what Tarell's saying

00:31:12.418 --> 00:31:13.710 line:90%
about bringing people with him.

00:31:13.710 --> 00:31:15.310 line:90%
But what do you tell--

00:31:15.310 --> 00:31:17.190 line:84%
what's the advice
for young people

00:31:17.190 --> 00:31:22.300 line:84%
who want to become filmmakers,
who want to tell their stories,

00:31:22.300 --> 00:31:25.470 line:90%
but don't know how to get there?

00:31:25.470 --> 00:31:27.750 line:90%
What advice can we give them?

00:31:27.750 --> 00:31:30.150 line:84%
Wow, I mean, they
are teaching me.

00:31:30.150 --> 00:31:35.040 line:84%
I mean, the great news is that
these young people are not

00:31:35.040 --> 00:31:38.190 line:84%
waiting around for Tarell
to tell them to do nothing.

00:31:41.840 --> 00:31:43.590 line:84%
[? Barry ?] talks about
this all the time.

00:31:43.590 --> 00:31:47.460 line:84%
With just their phones, even
the ones that aren't the latest

00:31:47.460 --> 00:31:48.840 line:90%
iPhone or--

00:31:48.840 --> 00:31:54.930 line:84%
I mean, they can film quality
film and/or digital film

00:31:54.930 --> 00:31:58.890 line:84%
and stream it and
get entertainment.

00:31:58.890 --> 00:32:04.190 line:84%
I've seen seven seconds of drama
that has, like, blown me away.

00:32:04.190 --> 00:32:05.660 line:90%
And I go on these--

00:32:05.660 --> 00:32:06.660 line:90%
you'll see me sometimes.

00:32:06.660 --> 00:32:08.070 line:84%
I'll go on these
little GoFundMes.

00:32:08.070 --> 00:32:10.490 line:84%
And they'll be like, GoFundMe,
I'm doing a 20-minute short.

00:32:10.490 --> 00:32:13.032 line:84%
And I'll be like, your 20-minute
short's going to be amazing.

00:32:13.032 --> 00:32:15.270 line:84%
Here is the $5 that
I've got for you

00:32:15.270 --> 00:32:16.980 line:90%
to keep investing and doing it.

00:32:16.980 --> 00:32:20.610 line:84%
And they've democratized
film in a way

00:32:20.610 --> 00:32:23.000 line:84%
that I know a lot of
people are scared.

00:32:23.000 --> 00:32:24.690 line:84%
I know a lot of
gatekeepers are scared.

00:32:24.690 --> 00:32:27.120 line:84%
And I think rather than
run away from them,

00:32:27.120 --> 00:32:29.760 line:90%
we have to embrace their voices.

00:32:29.760 --> 00:32:31.440 line:84%
We have to embrace
what they have to say

00:32:31.440 --> 00:32:32.607 line:90%
and how they want to say it.

00:32:32.607 --> 00:32:34.030 line:90%
They're showing us new ways.

00:32:34.030 --> 00:32:35.610 line:90%
They're showing us new methods.

00:32:35.610 --> 00:32:38.152 line:90%
And I'm excited about it.

00:32:38.152 --> 00:32:40.110 line:84%
I don't know that I have
to tell them anything.

00:32:40.110 --> 00:32:42.900 line:84%
I think the job is what
Donny Hathaway said.

00:32:42.900 --> 00:32:45.060 line:84%
Teach them and let
them lead the way.

00:32:45.060 --> 00:32:45.810 line:90%
I love that.

00:32:45.810 --> 00:32:49.620 line:84%
I just wonder with that,
though, if we have an old guard

00:32:49.620 --> 00:32:54.600 line:84%
Hollywood, how do we get those
young, avant-garde, radical

00:32:54.600 --> 00:33:00.900 line:84%
filmmakers in front of these
other places to get money,

00:33:00.900 --> 00:33:03.900 line:84%
to get opportunities,
to be able to be seen?

00:33:03.900 --> 00:33:07.278 line:84%
Well, again, Emil,
it's not your gift.

00:33:07.278 --> 00:33:08.070 line:90%
It's not your gift.

00:33:08.070 --> 00:33:09.150 line:90%
It's your job.

00:33:09.150 --> 00:33:13.650 line:84%
It's my duty to seek
out these talents.

00:33:13.650 --> 00:33:15.600 line:84%
There's only so
many stories I have.

00:33:15.600 --> 00:33:17.760 line:84%
There's only so many stories
that I can even write

00:33:17.760 --> 00:33:18.870 line:90%
or I can be--

00:33:18.870 --> 00:33:19.440 line:90%
I can direct.

00:33:19.440 --> 00:33:20.970 line:90%
There's only so many.

00:33:20.970 --> 00:33:25.950 line:84%
But if you are seeking
out, then you'll find.

00:33:25.950 --> 00:33:27.960 line:90%
The internet is crazy.

00:33:27.960 --> 00:33:31.200 line:90%
I agree, Tarell.

00:33:31.200 --> 00:33:34.830 line:84%
I have cast more people,
I have hired more people,

00:33:34.830 --> 00:33:37.755 line:90%
from Instagram and from Twitter.

00:33:41.510 --> 00:33:43.970 line:84%
I would've never
thought that that

00:33:43.970 --> 00:33:48.280 line:90%
is the way now to find talent.

00:33:48.280 --> 00:33:50.290 line:90%
And they're unafraid.

00:33:50.290 --> 00:33:53.740 line:90%
There aren't any rules.

00:33:53.740 --> 00:33:57.520 line:84%
You're drawn in like, what
the hell, who thinks of this.

00:33:57.520 --> 00:33:58.880 line:90%
It's good.

00:33:58.880 --> 00:33:59.810 line:90%
It's good.

00:33:59.810 --> 00:34:02.460 line:90%
Do you feel like marketers and--

00:34:02.460 --> 00:34:04.790 line:84%
But to your question,
Emil, about the old guard,

00:34:04.790 --> 00:34:05.870 line:90%
I mean, I don't know--

00:34:05.870 --> 00:34:08.630 line:84%
I mean, again, we
spend so much time--

00:34:08.630 --> 00:34:12.770 line:84%
I did this play called
Joan Jett Blakk for--

00:34:12.770 --> 00:34:14.790 line:84%
Ms. Blakk for
President in Chicago,

00:34:14.790 --> 00:34:19.219 line:84%
which is based on the life
of Terence Alan Smith, who

00:34:19.219 --> 00:34:24.650 line:84%
was Joan Jett Blakk, who got
on the floor of the 1992 DNC

00:34:24.650 --> 00:34:26.870 line:90%
in drag to run--

00:34:26.870 --> 00:34:31.550 line:84%
to launch a campaign for
Queer Nation for president

00:34:31.550 --> 00:34:33.409 line:84%
of the United States--
as far as we know,

00:34:33.409 --> 00:34:35.960 line:84%
the first Black drag queen to
run for president of the United

00:34:35.960 --> 00:34:37.830 line:90%
States.

00:34:37.830 --> 00:34:42.280 line:84%
And I was shocked and didn't
know anything about this story.

00:34:42.280 --> 00:34:44.840 line:84%
Found it, did this
play about it.

00:34:44.840 --> 00:34:49.730 line:84%
Found Terence Alan Smith living
in San Francisco and was just,

00:34:49.730 --> 00:34:53.600 line:84%
again, shocked at the
history that is lost.

00:34:53.600 --> 00:34:56.929 line:84%
I mean, their whole platform was
talking about the AIDS epidemic

00:34:56.929 --> 00:35:02.810 line:84%
at the time that was being
largely quelled or quieted

00:35:02.810 --> 00:35:05.570 line:84%
by the government, not
unlike what's happening today

00:35:05.570 --> 00:35:09.570 line:84%
in terms of COVID cases and
who's bearing the brunt of it

00:35:09.570 --> 00:35:12.350 line:84%
and whose communities
are really losing.

00:35:12.350 --> 00:35:18.740 line:84%
Anyway, the two things
that happened to me

00:35:18.740 --> 00:35:24.110 line:84%
were, one, I was once again
reminded of a generational loss

00:35:24.110 --> 00:35:27.380 line:84%
that we are still
suffering, grieving,

00:35:27.380 --> 00:35:29.180 line:90%
trying to find healing from.

00:35:29.180 --> 00:35:32.060 line:90%
There are so many between--

00:35:32.060 --> 00:35:35.330 line:84%
just a little older
than Lee, there's

00:35:35.330 --> 00:35:36.990 line:84%
so many men in
that generation who

00:35:36.990 --> 00:35:39.620 line:90%
were lost to the AIDS epidemic.

00:35:39.620 --> 00:35:42.680 line:90%
And that's so much talent gone.

00:35:42.680 --> 00:35:46.580 line:84%
And so the kind of passing
on that Lee talks about

00:35:46.580 --> 00:35:48.260 line:90%
isn't there, one.

00:35:48.260 --> 00:35:53.540 line:84%
But, two, there is this moment
where you have to stop--

00:35:53.540 --> 00:35:55.587 line:84%
well, I had to,
not anybody else.

00:35:55.587 --> 00:35:57.150 line:90%
It was a breakthrough moment.

00:35:57.150 --> 00:35:58.920 line:90%
I speak from a place that I--

00:35:58.920 --> 00:36:02.220 line:84%
I really had to stop trying
to think about, well,

00:36:02.220 --> 00:36:05.810 line:84%
how do I make this nice
enough for the old guard

00:36:05.810 --> 00:36:08.300 line:90%
to look at it.

00:36:08.300 --> 00:36:13.040 line:84%
I really had to go, I want
to spend the rest of my life

00:36:13.040 --> 00:36:18.410 line:84%
aiming all of my energy and
love and nurturing to people

00:36:18.410 --> 00:36:22.130 line:90%
I actually love and care about.

00:36:22.130 --> 00:36:24.760 line:84%
There was this
woman, this activist,

00:36:24.760 --> 00:36:30.460 line:84%
who calls herself
a D-word activist.

00:36:30.460 --> 00:36:32.060 line:90%
That's what she called herself.

00:36:32.060 --> 00:36:34.820 line:84%
And she was like, I spent the
first years of my life fighting

00:36:34.820 --> 00:36:36.560 line:90%
for people to--

00:36:36.560 --> 00:36:40.890 line:84%
fighting people who hated
me to try to love me.

00:36:40.890 --> 00:36:43.160 line:84%
And that doesn't really
make sense after a while.

00:36:43.160 --> 00:36:44.320 line:90%
You get to an age--

00:36:44.320 --> 00:36:45.148 line:90%
I'm about to be 40.

00:36:45.148 --> 00:36:46.940 line:84%
I'm like, I don't want
to spend the next 40

00:36:46.940 --> 00:36:50.840 line:84%
years of my life, 20 years of my
life aiming my energy at people

00:36:50.840 --> 00:36:54.020 line:84%
who already hate me or
don't care to love me.

00:36:54.020 --> 00:36:56.420 line:84%
I want to aim my energy at
people who do care to love me.

00:36:56.420 --> 00:36:58.503 line:84%
I want to aim my energy
at people I love and care,

00:36:58.503 --> 00:37:00.680 line:84%
and I want to wrap my
nurturing around them.

00:37:00.680 --> 00:37:05.900 line:84%
So I think that's a part of
this awakening for me, when

00:37:05.900 --> 00:37:07.160 line:90%
we talk about "woke."

00:37:07.160 --> 00:37:09.680 line:84%
My wokeness has more to
do with, I just got to--

00:37:09.680 --> 00:37:13.190 line:84%
I want to love on people,
and I want to nurture people.

00:37:13.190 --> 00:37:15.860 line:84%
Even if we've talked
shit about each other

00:37:15.860 --> 00:37:18.050 line:84%
before, behind each
other's back, that's cool.

00:37:18.050 --> 00:37:20.120 line:90%
That's over there.

00:37:20.120 --> 00:37:21.170 line:90%
Are you OK?

00:37:21.170 --> 00:37:22.490 line:90%
Are you strong?

00:37:22.490 --> 00:37:23.540 line:90%
Are you eating well?

00:37:23.540 --> 00:37:27.380 line:84%
Are you seeking
the help you need?

00:37:27.380 --> 00:37:30.440 line:84%
Are your talents
being given the space

00:37:30.440 --> 00:37:34.130 line:84%
and means to do what they can
do to add to the conversation,

00:37:34.130 --> 00:37:35.750 line:90%
or are you being quieted?

00:37:35.750 --> 00:37:38.570 line:84%
Because sometimes we see
our brothers and sisters

00:37:38.570 --> 00:37:41.900 line:84%
in real pain and
think because they're

00:37:41.900 --> 00:37:45.170 line:84%
acting out in those ways
that that's who they are.

00:37:45.170 --> 00:37:47.420 line:84%
And it really is
that they're in pain.

00:37:47.420 --> 00:37:51.260 line:84%
And we got to go in and be
like, yo, I see you in pain.

00:37:51.260 --> 00:37:53.973 line:84%
May I be of help as
much as I can be.

00:37:53.973 --> 00:37:56.390 line:84%
Or do you just need me to get
out of your way for a while?

00:37:56.390 --> 00:37:57.800 line:90%
And that's cool, too.

00:37:57.800 --> 00:37:59.100 line:90%
But I don't know.

00:37:59.100 --> 00:38:05.540 line:84%
I get tired of trying to aim
my energy at white people, who

00:38:05.540 --> 00:38:06.740 line:90%
may not--

00:38:06.740 --> 00:38:08.930 line:84%
if they don't choose to
love us at this point,

00:38:08.930 --> 00:38:10.880 line:84%
what am I going
to do about that?

00:38:10.880 --> 00:38:12.050 line:90%
Even white gay people.

00:38:12.050 --> 00:38:14.120 line:84%
We're always talking
about the community.

00:38:14.120 --> 00:38:16.010 line:84%
Well, there a lot
of white gay people

00:38:16.010 --> 00:38:17.440 line:90%
who don't like Black people.

00:38:20.380 --> 00:38:22.420 line:90%
You want me--

00:38:22.420 --> 00:38:24.920 line:84%
I'm not going to go and beat
nobody down because they're not

00:38:24.920 --> 00:38:26.210 line:90%
checking for me.

00:38:26.210 --> 00:38:27.450 line:90%
That's cool.

00:38:27.450 --> 00:38:29.030 line:84%
But where are those
little Black kids

00:38:29.030 --> 00:38:32.180 line:84%
who are growing up learning
that it's OK to hate themselves?

00:38:32.180 --> 00:38:35.030 line:90%
How do I get to them?

00:38:35.030 --> 00:38:38.580 line:90%
That's way more important to me.

00:38:38.580 --> 00:38:39.080 line:90%
Yeah.

00:38:39.080 --> 00:38:42.680 line:84%
And I feel like you have done an
extraordinary job of doing that

00:38:42.680 --> 00:38:47.150 line:84%
with Moonlight, with David
Makes Man, of telling stories

00:38:47.150 --> 00:38:50.570 line:84%
of people that could have
been just forgotten and lost

00:38:50.570 --> 00:38:53.180 line:84%
but actually had
something in their spirit

00:38:53.180 --> 00:38:55.010 line:84%
that they wanted
to push forward.

00:38:55.010 --> 00:38:58.730 line:84%
And I think that is-- those
are the important stories that

00:38:58.730 --> 00:39:02.150 line:84%
we need to tell right now,
is what are the stories that

00:39:02.150 --> 00:39:03.530 line:90%
have not been told.

00:39:03.530 --> 00:39:05.960 line:84%
Who are the young people
that are growing up

00:39:05.960 --> 00:39:07.850 line:90%
against crazy odds?

00:39:07.850 --> 00:39:10.670 line:84%
And how do we
amplify their stories

00:39:10.670 --> 00:39:12.260 line:90%
and change the narrative?

00:39:12.260 --> 00:39:17.930 line:84%
Faces, giving a face to
people that aren't seen

00:39:17.930 --> 00:39:20.990 line:84%
and a voice to people
that aren't heard.

00:39:20.990 --> 00:39:22.910 line:84%
And sometimes you've
got to be prepared

00:39:22.910 --> 00:39:24.740 line:90%
to take the criticism for that.

00:39:24.740 --> 00:39:28.567 line:84%
A lot of criticism for Monster's
Ball, an incredible amount

00:39:28.567 --> 00:39:29.900 line:90%
of criticism for Monster's Ball.

00:39:29.900 --> 00:39:33.680 line:84%
An incredible amount of
criticism for Precious.

00:39:33.680 --> 00:39:35.510 line:90%
Just why, Lee?

00:39:35.510 --> 00:39:37.550 line:84%
Why do you have to
show us in this light?

00:39:40.810 --> 00:39:42.590 line:90%
But that is our story.

00:39:42.590 --> 00:39:47.390 line:84%
Because who else is
going to if I don't?

00:39:47.390 --> 00:39:50.370 line:84%
So I'm going to bring
Shawn in because we're

00:39:50.370 --> 00:39:53.860 line:84%
going to add him to ask
a few more questions

00:39:53.860 --> 00:39:56.800 line:90%
and into the conversation.

00:39:56.800 --> 00:39:59.750 line:90%
Lee, Tarell, and Emil, wow.

00:39:59.750 --> 00:40:00.943 line:90%
Thank you so much for this.

00:40:00.943 --> 00:40:02.360 line:84%
Thank you so much
for your honesty

00:40:02.360 --> 00:40:04.980 line:84%
and for your transparency
and for sharing your story.

00:40:04.980 --> 00:40:06.980 line:84%
I've been listening to
everything that you said.

00:40:06.980 --> 00:40:09.047 line:90%
I had two questions.

00:40:09.047 --> 00:40:10.630 line:84%
One, with everything
that you all have

00:40:10.630 --> 00:40:13.430 line:90%
said, where do we go from here?

00:40:13.430 --> 00:40:15.470 line:84%
Where do we as a
community go from here,

00:40:15.470 --> 00:40:19.190 line:84%
working more collaboratively
together in this industry?

00:40:19.190 --> 00:40:23.690 line:84%
And where can we go as
filmmakers and as artists?

00:40:23.690 --> 00:40:26.480 line:84%
Where do we go from here with
everything you guys mentioned?

00:40:26.480 --> 00:40:29.020 line:90%
Right here is a great start.

00:40:29.020 --> 00:40:31.900 line:84%
Where we are right now, in
this space, the fact that we're

00:40:31.900 --> 00:40:35.050 line:84%
having and we're able to have
this conversation and a candid

00:40:35.050 --> 00:40:37.450 line:84%
conversation as
gay men, all of us,

00:40:37.450 --> 00:40:39.760 line:84%
as Black gay men,
all of us, I don't

00:40:39.760 --> 00:40:43.300 line:84%
know that this would have been
heard of through the Academy.

00:40:43.300 --> 00:40:46.000 line:84%
So, Shawn, thank you for
putting this together.

00:40:46.000 --> 00:40:47.990 line:84%
Because where do
we go from here?

00:40:47.990 --> 00:40:51.370 line:84%
We go with people that are
thinking like you, forward

00:40:51.370 --> 00:40:55.600 line:84%
thinkers like you, that are
going to be able to even give

00:40:55.600 --> 00:40:57.370 line:84%
me the courage to
come out and talk

00:40:57.370 --> 00:40:59.710 line:84%
about these things
for the world to see.

00:40:59.710 --> 00:41:04.180 line:84%
And these are private thoughts
that I had been holding on to,

00:41:04.180 --> 00:41:06.440 line:84%
I'm sure Tarell has
been holding on to, too.

00:41:09.360 --> 00:41:11.580 line:90%
But the truth will set you free.

00:41:11.580 --> 00:41:13.830 line:84%
Emil, where do you
feel as though,

00:41:13.830 --> 00:41:16.170 line:84%
with your extensive
experience in media

00:41:16.170 --> 00:41:18.570 line:84%
and understand that
media is a tool--

00:41:18.570 --> 00:41:21.670 line:84%
where do you believe
we can go from here?

00:41:21.670 --> 00:41:24.270 line:84%
Well, I have to
echo Lee's thoughts.

00:41:24.270 --> 00:41:26.490 line:84%
I mean, having
these conversations,

00:41:26.490 --> 00:41:30.300 line:84%
this is where we start, to
have a conversation with four

00:41:30.300 --> 00:41:33.900 line:84%
Black gay men about these
topics and these issues

00:41:33.900 --> 00:41:36.630 line:84%
and these real things
that affect our lives.

00:41:36.630 --> 00:41:39.090 line:90%
I think that is where we begin.

00:41:39.090 --> 00:41:46.140 line:84%
And I am so inspired by both
Lee and Tarell and the work

00:41:46.140 --> 00:41:50.820 line:84%
that you've done, because
you have just pushed away

00:41:50.820 --> 00:41:56.700 line:84%
the old guard and what we would
think Black gay men look like

00:41:56.700 --> 00:41:58.200 line:90%
or how they're depicted.

00:41:58.200 --> 00:42:01.540 line:84%
And you're showing real
humanity and real stories.

00:42:01.540 --> 00:42:03.118 line:84%
And I think that's
the beginning,

00:42:03.118 --> 00:42:04.410 line:90%
and that's where we need to go.

00:42:04.410 --> 00:42:08.040 line:84%
And we need to tap in and let
all these young people have

00:42:08.040 --> 00:42:10.610 line:84%
the opportunity to
tell their stories.

00:42:10.610 --> 00:42:11.110 line:90%
I love that.

00:42:11.110 --> 00:42:14.390 line:84%
And, Tarell, where
do we go from here?

00:42:14.390 --> 00:42:20.520 line:84%
You know, look, I think the
sky's the limit in terms

00:42:20.520 --> 00:42:21.390 line:90%
of where we can go.

00:42:21.390 --> 00:42:24.600 line:90%
I think we--

00:42:24.600 --> 00:42:28.260 line:84%
I hope we go a little
inside for a little bit,

00:42:28.260 --> 00:42:31.800 line:84%
meaning I hope we do a
whole bunch of healing,

00:42:31.800 --> 00:42:35.200 line:84%
a whole bunch of just
introspection, a whole bunch

00:42:35.200 --> 00:42:37.570 line:90%
of uplifting inside.

00:42:37.570 --> 00:42:41.190 line:84%
And I know that may sound a
little kumbaya, a little hold

00:42:41.190 --> 00:42:42.900 line:90%
hands and jump together.

00:42:42.900 --> 00:42:48.270 line:84%
But I really-- again,
I thrive on community.

00:42:48.270 --> 00:42:53.880 line:84%
And I know for our young people,
for our young LGBTQ folks

00:42:53.880 --> 00:42:57.060 line:84%
who are Black to survive,
they need community.

00:42:57.060 --> 00:42:59.185 line:90%
They need elders.

00:42:59.185 --> 00:43:00.810 line:84%
They need to know
the function of them,

00:43:00.810 --> 00:43:03.120 line:84%
but they also need to
know that there is space.

00:43:03.120 --> 00:43:05.490 line:84%
And there's a space in
there of love for them.

00:43:05.490 --> 00:43:07.530 line:84%
And that's the way
we get great art.

00:43:07.530 --> 00:43:10.488 line:84%
That's the way we get the
most compelling stuff.

00:43:10.488 --> 00:43:12.030 line:84%
I mean, sometimes
you actually get it

00:43:12.030 --> 00:43:16.290 line:90%
by pressuring somebody to do--

00:43:16.290 --> 00:43:20.670 line:90%
take the by any means necessary.

00:43:20.670 --> 00:43:22.650 line:84%
And you get good
art that way, too.

00:43:22.650 --> 00:43:25.830 line:90%
But also, what could we get?

00:43:25.830 --> 00:43:29.760 line:84%
Imagine right now
all the young artists

00:43:29.760 --> 00:43:34.560 line:84%
who are queer and Black got
and felt protected enough

00:43:34.560 --> 00:43:39.090 line:84%
and safe enough to
imagine, without thinking

00:43:39.090 --> 00:43:41.280 line:84%
about race, without
thinking about sexuality,

00:43:41.280 --> 00:43:43.610 line:90%
but just imagine what--

00:43:43.610 --> 00:43:44.520 line:90%
imagine, period.

00:43:44.520 --> 00:43:46.495 line:84%
If their brain isn't
thinking about survival,

00:43:46.495 --> 00:43:48.120 line:84%
what else could they
be thinking about?

00:43:48.120 --> 00:43:52.290 line:84%
What dreams may come
if they had the ability

00:43:52.290 --> 00:43:55.710 line:84%
to stop thinking about
their own untimely death

00:43:55.710 --> 00:43:57.460 line:90%
every third thought?

00:43:57.460 --> 00:43:58.690 line:90%
That's what I want for them.

00:43:58.690 --> 00:44:00.120 line:90%
I want that for them.

00:44:00.120 --> 00:44:01.020 line:90%
Thank you.

00:44:01.020 --> 00:44:05.610 line:84%
And the last question is,
you all are very magical,

00:44:05.610 --> 00:44:06.930 line:90%
each one of you.

00:44:06.930 --> 00:44:09.950 line:84%
Tell me a time when you
started to realize your magic.

00:44:09.950 --> 00:44:11.370 line:84%
And, Emil, we can
start with you.

00:44:11.370 --> 00:44:12.840 line:90%
When did you realize your magic?

00:44:12.840 --> 00:44:15.990 line:84%
When did you realize that all
of the things that are you

00:44:15.990 --> 00:44:19.146 line:84%
are adding value to you and
everyone else around you?

00:44:19.146 --> 00:44:25.710 line:84%
I think for me a lot of the time
that I spent at Vibe magazine

00:44:25.710 --> 00:44:30.780 line:84%
really gave me the opportunity
to understand that I could

00:44:30.780 --> 00:44:34.680 line:84%
create space for voices that
weren't going to be heard,

00:44:34.680 --> 00:44:39.870 line:84%
for stories that were
unheard of, and also

00:44:39.870 --> 00:44:47.190 line:84%
to present imagery of Black
people like rock stars,

00:44:47.190 --> 00:44:50.910 line:84%
like the way that we
really see ourselves

00:44:50.910 --> 00:44:53.790 line:84%
but the world doesn't
see us like that.

00:44:53.790 --> 00:44:55.650 line:90%
I think that was the first time.

00:44:55.650 --> 00:44:59.560 line:84%
And then every day with the work
with Native Son, I'm inspired.

00:44:59.560 --> 00:45:02.040 line:84%
I mean, to be able
to see this come

00:45:02.040 --> 00:45:05.610 line:84%
to life and this
conversation and to sit

00:45:05.610 --> 00:45:07.950 line:84%
with Lee and Tarell
and yourself,

00:45:07.950 --> 00:45:10.920 line:90%
I think this is beyond us.

00:45:10.920 --> 00:45:13.620 line:84%
And I think about what
Lee said when it becomes,

00:45:13.620 --> 00:45:15.090 line:90%
it's a God moment.

00:45:15.090 --> 00:45:16.710 line:90%
It's a community moment.

00:45:16.710 --> 00:45:23.190 line:84%
And it is about creating these
spaces and these conversations.

00:45:23.190 --> 00:45:23.690 line:90%
Thank you.

00:45:23.690 --> 00:45:25.220 line:84%
And then, Tarell,
what about you?

00:45:25.220 --> 00:45:26.928 line:84%
When did you start to
realize your magic?

00:45:29.980 --> 00:45:34.570 line:84%
Well, it was a little bit of
rough magic or practical magic,

00:45:34.570 --> 00:45:38.020 line:84%
because one time
I just thought--

00:45:38.020 --> 00:45:41.050 line:84%
my mother had me when
she was 16 years old.

00:45:41.050 --> 00:45:46.570 line:84%
And I think I was
about 12 or 13,

00:45:46.570 --> 00:45:51.160 line:84%
but I just kept trying to
imagine her life if she didn't

00:45:51.160 --> 00:45:54.050 line:84%
have a kid at 16
or 17, like what

00:45:54.050 --> 00:45:55.300 line:90%
her life would have been like.

00:45:55.300 --> 00:46:02.775 line:84%
She was really smart, really
beautiful, and charming,

00:46:02.775 --> 00:46:04.650 line:84%
had all those things
that a model could have.

00:46:04.650 --> 00:46:05.800 line:90%
She was 6 feet tall.

00:46:05.800 --> 00:46:07.350 line:90%
She was slender.

00:46:07.350 --> 00:46:10.720 line:84%
And I was just like, your
life would have been great

00:46:10.720 --> 00:46:14.170 line:90%
if you just didn't have a kid.

00:46:14.170 --> 00:46:15.190 line:90%
And she slapped me.

00:46:17.830 --> 00:46:22.040 line:84%
As we say in Liberty City, she
slapped fire out of my face.

00:46:22.040 --> 00:46:26.800 line:84%
And she was like, if
I didn't want you,

00:46:26.800 --> 00:46:29.190 line:90%
I wouldn't have had you.

00:46:29.190 --> 00:46:31.800 line:84%
And it just made
me think in that--

00:46:31.800 --> 00:46:33.300 line:90%
I mean, I know it sounds weird.

00:46:33.300 --> 00:46:37.470 line:84%
But in that moment, I was like,
oh, no, there was purpose.

00:46:37.470 --> 00:46:38.970 line:90%
She had me on purpose.

00:46:38.970 --> 00:46:40.930 line:90%
I am here for a purpose.

00:46:40.930 --> 00:46:44.730 line:84%
Like, this wasn't an
accidental sort of slip-up.

00:46:44.730 --> 00:46:48.990 line:84%
She was like, no, no, you
were brought here on purpose.

00:46:48.990 --> 00:46:51.960 line:84%
And I remember from that day
on, I just sort of walked

00:46:51.960 --> 00:46:54.510 line:84%
around going, oh, no,
I'm here for something.

00:46:54.510 --> 00:46:57.540 line:84%
There's something
here I'm called to do.

00:46:57.540 --> 00:47:00.690 line:84%
And it just shifted my
view of things radically.

00:47:00.690 --> 00:47:02.250 line:84%
Even in my most
depressing moments,

00:47:02.250 --> 00:47:05.130 line:84%
I can still kind
of feel that slap

00:47:05.130 --> 00:47:07.740 line:84%
and remember that, no,
I'm here for something.

00:47:07.740 --> 00:47:09.150 line:90%
I'm here for a reason.

00:47:09.150 --> 00:47:10.375 line:90%
Wow.

00:47:10.375 --> 00:47:11.790 line:90%
Wow, Tarell.

00:47:11.790 --> 00:47:15.230 line:90%
My mom had me when she was 18.

00:47:15.230 --> 00:47:17.840 line:90%
Wow.

00:47:17.840 --> 00:47:21.950 line:90%
For me it was the--

00:47:21.950 --> 00:47:26.120 line:84%
I think the first couple
of episodes on Empire.

00:47:28.660 --> 00:47:31.672 line:84%
In trying to find your
voice, you don't know--

00:47:31.672 --> 00:47:33.630 line:84%
you know you're an artist,
but you don't really

00:47:33.630 --> 00:47:34.890 line:90%
know what your worth is.

00:47:34.890 --> 00:47:38.518 line:90%
And it was also--

00:47:38.518 --> 00:47:39.060 line:90%
I don't know.

00:47:39.060 --> 00:47:40.980 line:90%
Let me just find myself.

00:47:40.980 --> 00:47:43.950 line:90%
I want to express myself.

00:47:43.950 --> 00:47:45.320 line:90%
But that's a sissy thing to do.

00:47:45.320 --> 00:47:46.820 line:84%
That's not really--
that's not work.

00:47:46.820 --> 00:47:47.880 line:90%
Is that work?

00:47:47.880 --> 00:47:49.530 line:90%
Is that real work?

00:47:49.530 --> 00:47:58.440 line:84%
And so my brother was
in jail during Empire.

00:47:58.440 --> 00:48:01.780 line:84%
And he's three years
younger than me.

00:48:01.780 --> 00:48:08.400 line:84%
And he was embarrassed
about who it was that I was.

00:48:08.400 --> 00:48:10.710 line:84%
And so when you live
with that embarrassment

00:48:10.710 --> 00:48:13.950 line:84%
from your brother
and your father,

00:48:13.950 --> 00:48:15.750 line:90%
you really think that you are--

00:48:15.750 --> 00:48:17.280 line:90%
it affects you deeply.

00:48:17.280 --> 00:48:18.000 line:90%
It affects you.

00:48:18.000 --> 00:48:18.900 line:90%
It affects you.

00:48:22.120 --> 00:48:23.830 line:90%
You are alone.

00:48:23.830 --> 00:48:29.760 line:84%
And three episodes--
we didn't talk.

00:48:29.760 --> 00:48:31.110 line:90%
We still don't really talk.

00:48:31.110 --> 00:48:35.595 line:84%
But three episodes
in, and the numbers

00:48:35.595 --> 00:48:36.970 line:84%
were just going
through the roof.

00:48:36.970 --> 00:48:40.150 line:84%
And people couldn't understand
why people were watching this.

00:48:40.150 --> 00:48:48.380 line:84%
I got a letter from him from
jail and he apologizing.

00:48:48.380 --> 00:48:51.480 line:90%
And I broke down.

00:48:51.480 --> 00:48:56.840 line:84%
And the biggest award that
I've ever gotten was from him.

00:48:56.840 --> 00:49:00.040 line:84%
And he said that
when Empire was on,

00:49:00.040 --> 00:49:04.090 line:90%
the entire prison shut down.

00:49:04.090 --> 00:49:06.440 line:90%
You could hear a pin drop.

00:49:06.440 --> 00:49:10.570 line:84%
3,000 men were glued
to that television.

00:49:10.570 --> 00:49:14.590 line:84%
And I wept when I
got that letter.

00:49:17.260 --> 00:49:17.840 line:90%
I wept.

00:49:17.840 --> 00:49:19.150 line:90%
I didn't return the--

00:49:19.150 --> 00:49:20.530 line:84%
I wanted to write
him back, but I

00:49:20.530 --> 00:49:23.290 line:84%
didn't know how
to write him back

00:49:23.290 --> 00:49:27.100 line:84%
because so much had gone--
we had gone through so much

00:49:27.100 --> 00:49:29.125 line:90%
by then, and we were in our 50s.

00:49:31.920 --> 00:49:37.390 line:84%
But I knew that I had a voice
and that everything that

00:49:37.390 --> 00:49:42.940 line:84%
had put me through, everything
that I'd been through,

00:49:42.940 --> 00:49:48.640 line:84%
that I was communicating to
3,000 men up in that prison.

00:49:48.640 --> 00:49:51.283 line:84%
And they were
focused on my word.

00:49:51.283 --> 00:49:52.450 line:90%
And that's a powerful thing.

00:49:52.450 --> 00:49:55.120 line:84%
I said, oh my god, I got
a little magic in me.

00:49:58.690 --> 00:50:01.600 line:84%
I think it's more than a
little magic, the three of you

00:50:01.600 --> 00:50:03.330 line:90%
on this call.

00:50:03.330 --> 00:50:05.950 line:84%
And I just wanted to say,
on behalf of the Academy

00:50:05.950 --> 00:50:07.470 line:84%
and the members
and everyone who's

00:50:07.470 --> 00:50:09.137 line:84%
going to be tuning
in and participating,

00:50:09.137 --> 00:50:11.440 line:84%
thank you so much
for lending or light,

00:50:11.440 --> 00:50:14.110 line:84%
your transparency, for
sharing your stories.

00:50:14.110 --> 00:50:16.120 line:84%
Emil, we can thank you
so much for moderating

00:50:16.120 --> 00:50:17.680 line:90%
this conversation.

00:50:17.680 --> 00:50:19.360 line:84%
Lee Daniels, as
always, thank you

00:50:19.360 --> 00:50:21.937 line:84%
so much for showing up
and being your true self.

00:50:21.937 --> 00:50:24.520 line:84%
Tarell Alvin McCraney, thank you
all so much for participating

00:50:24.520 --> 00:50:26.160 line:90%
in Academy Dialogues.

00:50:26.160 --> 00:50:27.340 line:90%
Thank you.

00:50:27.340 --> 00:50:29.100 line:90%
Thank you.

